    declaration: enum-decl #156 11041:187:1-11128:192:1 name="@2::Answer" type-parameters=[] doc="What an `App` returns for a request. `Whole` is a whole response, as a `Handler` returns. `Stream` is a response\nwhose body arrives on a channel. `Upgrade` is what `accept_websocket` makes: its\n`Accept` runs a WebSocket on the connection, and its `Reply` (a request that is no handshake) is sent as `Whole`."
      variant: enum-variant #151 11055:188:3-11080:189:1 name="Whole"
        payload: type-ref #150 11061:188:9-11078:188:26 name="HttpWire.Response"
      variant: enum-variant #153 11082:189:3-11105:190:1 name="Stream"
        payload: type-ref #152 11089:189:10-11103:189:24 name="@2::StreamResponse"
      variant: enum-variant #155 11107:190:3-11124:191:1 name="Upgrade"
        payload: type-ref #154 11115:190:11-11122:190:18 name="@2::Upgrade"
    declaration: type-alias-decl #162 11459:196:1-11520:197:1 name="@2::App" doc="The function that `serve` and `serve_tls` call for each request: the request with an empty `body`, the\n`RequestBody` that carries the body as it arrives, and the `Answer` out. A route that wants the body whole calls\n`read_all`; `app` makes an `App` from a `Handler`. A middleware is a function from an `App` to an `App`."
      target: type-ref #161 11470:196:12-11519:196:61 name="Fn"
        arg: type-ref #157 11473:196:15-11489:196:31 name="HttpWire.Request"
        arg: type-ref #158 11491:196:33-11502:196:44 name="@2::RequestBody"
        arg: type-ref #160 11504:196:46-11518:196:60 name="Result"
          arg: type-ref #159 11511:196:53-11517:196:59 name="@2::Answer"
    declaration: function-decl #186 13153:215:1-13340:224:1 name="@2::proxy_from_env" doc="The proxy that the environment names for `url`, to pass as `HTTP.ClientOptions(proxy: ...)`, or `\"\"` when there is\nnone. It is the only call that reads the environment: a client never does, so nothing changes until a program asks.\nIt follows curl. An `http://` URL reads `http_proxy` and an `https://` URL `https_proxy`; the lower-case name wins\nover `HTTP_PROXY` or `HTTPS_PROXY`, even when it is set and empty (which means no proxy). `HTTP_PROXY` is ignored\nwhen `REQUEST_METHOD` is set, as in a CGI program, where a request's `Proxy` header becomes it (httpoxy); on\nWindows, whose names do not differ by case, an `http://` URL then gets no proxy. `ALL_PROXY` is not read. A value\nwith no `://` is read as `http://` (`proxy.example:8080`), and one with another scheme is returned as it is, so the\nclient that gets it says it is not an `http://` origin.\n\n`no_proxy` (or `NO_PROXY`) is a list, separated by commas or spaces, that says where no proxy is used, and the\nresult is then `\"\"`: `*` is every host; `example.com` and `.example.com` are that domain and its subdomains; an\nentry may end in `:port` (`[::1]:8080` for an IPv6 literal); an IP literal matches only itself, and an IPv4 range\nsuch as `10.0.0.0/8` matches its addresses (an IPv6 range is not read). Empty entries and an entry that is not\nvalid are ignored, one trailing dot of a host or an entry does not count, and the case of a host is not\nsignificant. A `url` that is not valid gives `\"\"`: the call that gets it reports it.\n\nAn `https://` URL gets `https_proxy`, which `HTTP` then reaches with a `CONNECT` tunnel."
      param: param #165 13172:215:20-13183:215:31 name="url"
        type: type-ref #164 13177:215:25-13183:215:31 name="String"
      result: type-ref #166 13188:215:36-13194:215:42 name="String"
      body: case-stmt #185 13197:216:3-13336:223:1
        value: call #168 13202:216:8-13216:216:22 name="@2::parse_url" piped=false
          arg: name-ref #167 13212:216:18-13215:216:21 name="url"
        arm: case-arm #180 13221:217:5-13311:221:1
          pattern: variant-pattern #169 13221:217:5-13230:217:14 enum="Result" variant="Ok" bindings=["where"]
          body: if-stmt #175 13240:218:7-13274:219:1 guard
            branch: if-branch #174 13240:218:7-13273:218:40
              condition: call #173 13253:218:20-13273:218:40 name="@2::no_proxy_for?" piped=false
                arg: name-ref #172 13267:218:34-13272:218:39 name="where"
              body: return-stmt #171 13240:218:7-13249:218:16
                value: literal #170 13247:218:14-13249:218:16 value=string:""
          body: return-stmt #179 13281:220:7-13310:220:36 implicit
